      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It is a common observation that in highly fractured societies, ethnic loyalty often serves as the primary organizing principle during times of stress. When national institutions are weak or perceived as biased, people naturally revert to the most reliable in-group identity for protection and solidarity. This tendency means that political mobilization frequently aligns along ethnic lines, making the baseline for social cohesion significantly more volatile than in more homogenous or strongly integrated societies.",
      "source": "target",
